      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="kicker">Immigration, at a high level</p>
        <h1>Buying a house does not let you stay.</h1>
        <p class="lede">People assume the deed is a visa. It is not. Residency is a separate process with its own rules, and those rules change. Nothing here is immigration advice or a path you can follow from a blog.</p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap measure">
        <h2>What a purchase does not do</h2>
        <p>A registered property does not give you residency, a work permit, or citizenship. I became a Peruvian citizen later, after a life here. That was an immigration process. It was not a prize for having bought land. I will not publish a do-it-yourself citizenship guide, because I am not qualified to maintain one and because my file is not your file.</p>
        <h2>Categories people ask about</h2>
        <p>These are names, not instructions. An immigration lawyer is the person who says whether you fit.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong><span lang="es">Rentista</span>.</strong> A residence path associated with a steady pension or other qualifying income. It is a common question for retirees. The income test and the paperwork are theirs to confirm.</li>
          <li><strong>Investor.</strong> A category tied to an investment that meets a threshold and a set of conditions. Buying an ordinary house is not automatically that category.</li>
          <li><strong>Work.</strong> Based on a job and an employer’s process, not on a property.</li>
          <li><strong>Family.</strong> Based on a qualifying relationship with a Peruvian or a resident.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A <span lang="es">carné de extranjería</span> is the foreigner ID card that comes with a resident status. It is not something the notary prints because you bought an apartment.</p>
        <h2>Overstaying</h2>
        <p>A tourist stay has a limit. Overstaying can mean fines and trouble the next time you enter or the next time you apply for something real. I am not describing how to manage an overstay. Leave on time or change status properly. If you are unsure of your date, ask an immigration lawyer or the authority, not a property guide.</p>
        <h2>Tourist stamp, carné, and citizen</h2>
        <p>I bought property when I was still a foreigner coming and going, and I bought again after I naturalized. The purchase did not change my immigration status in either case. What felt different, in ordinary life, was administration. Banks, longer stays, and signing as a citizen were not the same experience as arranging documents as a visitor. A person with a <span lang="es">carné de extranjería</span> sits between those two: resident ID, not citizenship.</p>
        <p>I am not going to invent the procedural steps of my own file as if they were current instructions. If you are on a tourist stamp, ask your property lawyer what identification the notary will accept for the <span lang="es">escritura</span>, and ask an immigration lawyer about how long you can stay. Those are different appointments.</p>
        <p>The border-zone limit on foreign ownership is one place citizenship mattered in substance. It is described on the <a href="legal.html#border">legal page</a>. It is still not a reason to buy something in the hope of becoming Peruvian.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
